      <section class="section" id="what-are">
        <h2>1. What Are Cosmetic Aerosol Products?</h2>
        <p>Cosmetic aerosol products are personal care formulations packaged in pressurized containers that dispense the product in the form of a spray, mist, foam, or mousse using a controlled propellant system. These products are classified as cosmetics under regulatory frameworks but are also governed by pressure vessel safety laws and hazardous goods transport regulations.</p>
        <p>From a chemical and process engineering perspective, cosmetic aerosols are not merely sprays; they are complex two- or three-phase thermodynamic systems designed to maintain pressure stability over a wide temperature range. Formulating these systems requires balancing active concentrations against propellant solubility. Below are the primary phases that comprise any standard cosmetic aerosol:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A cosmetic formulation (liquid or semi-liquid concentrate containing actives and carriers)</li>
          <li>A propellant phase (liquefied hydrocarbon gas or compressed gas)</li>
          <li>A precision valve–actuator assembly that controls flow and discharge geometry</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Pressurized dispensing offers distinct technical and sensory advantages over standard pump bottles and creams. By excluding ambient air and moisture, the container maintains active ingredient efficacy and prevents contamination throughout the product's lifespan. The primary benefits of utilizing aerosol delivery in personal care include:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Uniform and reproducible application across hair and skin surfaces</li>
          <li>Hygienic, non-contact usage that prevents product contamination</li>
          <li>Superior consumer experience with rapid-drying fine sprays and dense foams</li>
          <li>Extended shelf stability in completely sealed systems</li>
          <li>Controlled dosing per actuation to optimize product efficiency</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

